# Deployment API absence handling — follow-up acceptance matrix

Recorded before implementation. This extends the bounded namespace guard to the
remaining text-based `is_not_found_error` callers in `scripts/deploy.sh`.
No behavior change or passing result is claimed by this matrix.

| Case | Production path | Required observation |
| --- | --- | --- |
| DA1 | `require_helm_release_idle` | Successful structured release observation distinguishes absent, idle and pending releases; failed lookup or malformed/ambiguous identity never admits Helm mutation. |
| DA2 | `ensure_tailscale_oauth_secret` | Existing secret remains untouched; only a successful, exact absent-resource observation permits the existing creation flow. Failed credentials/API/discovery, including misleading `not found` text, never trigger secret apply. |
| DA3 | Namespaced, cluster and manifested resource deletion helpers | Successful exact absence is idempotent; successful presence admits the intended existing deletion. Failed or invalid lookup stops rather than claiming absence or completion. |
| DA4 | `uninstall_helm_release_if_present` | Successful exact absence is idempotent; presence admits uninstall; lookup failure cannot claim removal. |
| DA5 | Optional status tables | Nonzero API results are reported as incomplete observations, never as a confirmed empty collection. |
| DA6 | All migrated callers | Remove the generic error-text classifier and competing interpretation paths. Validate command status before interpreting stdout, including nonzero commands that emit plausible valid JSON. |
| DA7 | Actual script entrypoints | Positive, empty, malformed, duplicate/wrong identity, pending and nonzero cases exercise the original orchestration with explicit CLI fixtures; meaningful mutations expose weakened guards. Any actual client/local HTTP probe remains identified as such, not a real cluster proof. |
| DA-DOC | Setup, deployment and cleanup instructions | Document required read/list permissions, exact absence/error behavior, rerun semantics and residual host/live requirements. Record changed docs and independent review. |

Inspect supported Helm/kubectl output before defining accepted structured shapes.
Do not suppress server-side errors with `--ignore-not-found` to manufacture an
empty result. Preserve existing ownership checks and deletion scope; this change
does not authorize running deployment, secret writes or deletion against a live
system. Real host preparation, installation and recovery remain separate H1–H3
obligations.

## Current implementation and independent finding

The first implementation inferred release absence/latest status from decoded
`helm list` output. Fresh review demonstrated with actual Helm 3.22.0 and a local
HTTP fixture that this is unsafe: corrupt records are silently skipped, so a sole
record appears absent and a corrupt latest record can expose an older deployed
revision. The earlier 6/51 fixture pass did not cover this client behavior and is
historical evidence only. That implementation was rejected and replaced.

DA8, recorded before correction: establish absence from a successful raw backend
collection; decode **every** observed revision through original Helm and reconcile
identity, revision and status before using the latest state. Corrupt sole, latest
and older records, mismatched decoded identity, and unsupported drivers must stop
mutation. Preserve Secret confidentiality and independently recheck actual clients.

The final `observe_helm_release` supports Helm Secret/ConfigMap storage explicitly,
including Helm's plural aliases. It lists records with `owner=helm,name=<release>`
and validates resource type, namespace, full record name, labels, nonempty payload
and unique positive safe-integer revision. A validated empty collection means
absent. All revisions are sorted oldest first and read with original
`helm status --revision N -o json`; decoded name, namespace, revision and status
must match the raw record. No duplicate release decoder, history cap or fallback
to a readable older revision was introduced. This costs one raw list plus one
Helm status read per stored revision. Busy/unknown deployment status remains denied.

All old `is_not_found_error` callers and that generic classifier are removed.
The small observation parser emits state tokens and fixed errors, never response
bodies. Resource lookups retain exact-kind/API/name/namespace validation. Helm
storage, generic resources and namespace setup accept only absent or empty-string
continuation tokens. Those parser-boundary checks reject incomplete or malformed
successful CLI output; actual kubectl ordinarily aggregates collection pages.

A shared-connection guard rejects nonempty Helm-only context/API/auth/TLS overrides
before cluster lookup in setup, deployment and teardown entrypoints. Both clients
must use the shared KUBECONFIG context. The supported override names were read
from Helm 3.22.0's actual EnvSettings source. Diagnostics name the setting without
printing its value. Driver validation prevents silent SQL/memory fallback.

The original GitOps ownership checks and mutation scope are retained. OAuth
creation propagates pipeline failure explicitly because its conditional caller
disables implicit Bash errexit. Optional status remains nonfatal but reports failed
observations as incomplete. This does not fix every other API call in the large
deploy script or separate ownership module. Observations remain point-in-time,
not locks against concurrent operators or arbitrary storage tampering outside
the selected backend's labelled release records.

## Evidence and boundaries

All author evidence is in `docs/review/evidence/deploy-api-errors-20260921/`.
The original `tests-final.txt` (6 methods/51 scenarios), original two mutant logs,
and original primary-source list investigation remain historical. Current results
are explicitly named `*storage-final*`, `actual-storage-client*` and
`namespace-actual-client-final.txt`; final counts are recorded below.

The CLI-fixture test executes original entrypoints `agent nova`, `tailscale`,
`teardown`, `teardown-agents` and `status`. kubectl/Helm are synthetic peers; only
release selection/rendering and infrastructure archive acquisition are replaced
at the Node boundary. Actual observation and GitOps ownership modules execute.
No real cluster or credential is used. The expanded tests retain all 51 earlier
scenarios and add history/storage, continuation and connection-override cases.

`actual-storage-client.py` exercises original `teardown-agents`, actual kubectl
collection reads, actual Helm revision GET/decoding and the actual parser against
a local HTTP fixture. GitOps discovery is a CLI fixture and uninstall only records
a requested action. It covers both backends with empty, valid, valid history,
403, corrupt sole/latest/older and wrong decoded identity cases. Full command
traces and HTTP request paths are retained; no actual uninstall is performed.
The original namespace reviewer's actual-client probe is rerun separately.

Primary source snapshots for configured Helm 3.18.4/3.22.0 list behavior are retained
alongside 3.22.0 storage decoder/backend selection and environment source. The
actual client executions use the installed Helm 3.22.0 and kubectl binaries; this
is not a live cluster or separate native execution of every supported Helm version.

DA-DOC: `docs/deployment/setup-flow.md` and
`docs/deployment/tailscale-operator.md` describe permissions, explicit storage and
connection contracts, error versus absence, all-history verification cost, safe
reruns and residual live requirements. Repair broken history through reviewed
recovery rather than deleting records to bypass the check. Earlier mutations may
remain after a later failure. Fresh independent final review is required; no
H1–H3 or finding-register closure is claimed.

Final correction results: **8/8 fixture test methods, all 83 original-entrypoint
scenarios passed** in 45.754 seconds (`tests-storage-final.txt`). The namespace
suite passed **18/18**, retaining its original 14 plus four invalid-continuation
cases (`bootstrap-storage-final.txt`). Actual kubectl/Helm with the local HTTP
storage fixture passed **16/16** across both backends
(`actual-storage-client-final.txt`, full trace `actual-storage-client.json`).
The original namespace actual-client fixture passed **5/5** again
(`namespace-actual-client-final.txt`). Installed client versions are retained.
Canonical parser ESLint, Bash syntax and changed-file whitespace checks passed.
`source-storage-final-sha256.json` binds final source/test/operator-doc identities.

Final negative controls run isolated copies. Skipping corrupt revision errors
causes three semantic assertion failures; checking only the newest revision
violates the full-history oracle. A separate original-entrypoint probe demonstrates
that the latest-only mutant actually admits Helm upgrade when an older record is
corrupt (`latest-only-corrupt-old-admission.txt`), whereas the unchanged suite's
same original scenario rejects it. The harness and named raw logs are retained.
The initial storage-mutant harness had a Python quoting error before running any
scenario; it is retained as `mutants-storage-initial-harness-error.txt` and is not
counted as mutation detection. No production file was mutated by these controls.
